# Smooths: geom_smooth

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mpg = Giggly.Datasets.mpg()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`geom_smooth()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/geom_smooth.html), which covers
`stat_smooth()` too, in the order the page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first
and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is
waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

`geom_smooth()` draws a smoothed line through the data, with a band for its confidence interval,
to help the eye see a pattern among overlapping points. `stat_smooth()` takes the same arguments,
for drawing the fit with another geom. Left to choose, it fits a local regression, R's `loess()`,
when the largest group has fewer than 1,000 rows, and otherwise a generalised additive model,
`mgcv::gam()`; `method` names any other modelling function, such as `lm()`. Giggly has both,
`Giggly.Plot.geom_smooth/3` and `Giggly.Plot.stat_smooth/3`, which fit a straight line by least
squares, a loess or a gam (`method: :lm`, `:loess` or `:gam`), choosing as ggplot2 does when no
method is given, and take `se:`, `level:`, `n:`, `span:`, `fullrange:` and `orientation:`. It has
no `formula` option, no `method.args` option, no other modelling function, such as `glm()`, and no
function as a method.

Giggly's loess fits the curve at each of its points directly, as R's `loess(surface = "direct")`
does; R's default fits at the corners of cells it divides the data into and joins those fits with
cubic pieces, so a loess curve here sits a point or two from R's, more where the span is small,
and each example says how far. Giggly's gam, from 1,000 rows, fixes a choice mgcv leaves to its
linear algebra's rounding, so its curve can sit up to about a thousandth of the y axis's range
from R's; no example here fits one. A plot is described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with
`Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the
cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws a plot and shows it below the cell
with `Kino.Image`. It binds `Giggly.Datasets.mpg/0`, ggplot2's `mpg`, to `mpg`, ggplot2's name for
it.

## A smooth through the points

```r
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth()
```

`mpg` has fewer than 1,000 rows, so the smooth is a `loess()` fit, with a band for its 95%
confidence interval. Giggly's curve sits up to 1.7 points from R's on this drawing, and its band's
edges up to 2.9.

```elixir
mpg
|> Plot.new(%{x: :displ, y: :hwy})
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> Plot.geom_smooth()
|> render.()
```

## Fitting along the y axis

```r
# If you need the fitting to be done along the y-axis set the orientation
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(orientation = "y")
```

The curve fits `displ` to `hwy`. Giggly's sits up to 1.7 points from R's, and its band's edges up
to 5.7.

```elixir
mpg
|> Plot.new(%{x: :displ, y: :hwy})
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> Plot.geom_smooth(orientation: :y)
|> render.()
```

## How wiggly the smooth is

```r
# Use span to control the "wiggliness" of the default loess smoother.
# The span is the fraction of points used to fit each local regression:
# small numbers make a wigglier curve, larger numbers make a smoother curve.
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(span = 0.3)
```

With a span this small, R's cells are small too, and its curve bends where Giggly's direct fit
does not: Giggly's sits up to 15 points from R's, among the smallest engines, and its band's edges
up to 18.

```elixir
mpg
|> Plot.new(%{x: :displ, y: :hwy})
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> Plot.geom_smooth(span: 0.3)
|> render.()
```

## Other models

```r
# Instead of a loess smooth, you can use any other modelling function:
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(method = lm, se = FALSE)

ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(method = lm, formula = y ~ splines::bs(x, 3), se = FALSE)
```

`se = FALSE` leaves out the confidence band. The second fits a linear model to a B-spline of `x`
from the `splines` package.

Giggly draws the first plot, where R's `method = lm` is `method: :lm`.

```elixir
mpg
|> Plot.new(%{x: :displ, y: :hwy})
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> Plot.geom_smooth(method: :lm, se: false)
|> render.()
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_smooth()`'s `formula` option, here a B-spline from the `splines` package.

## A smooth for each group

```r
# Smooths are automatically fit to each group (defined by categorical
# aesthetics or the group aesthetic) and for each facet.

ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy, colour = class)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(se = FALSE, method = lm)
```

Mapping `colour` to `class` makes a group of each class, and a line is fitted to each.

```elixir
mpg
|> Plot.new(%{x: :displ, y: :hwy, colour: :class})
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> Plot.geom_smooth(se: false, method: :lm)
|> render.()
```

## A smooth in each panel

```r
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(span = 0.8) +
  facet_wrap(~drv)
```

`facet_wrap(~drv)` is `Giggly.Plot.facet_wrap(plot, :drv)`. Giggly's curves sit up to 7.8 points
from R's, in the panel of rear-wheel-drive cars, the fewest, and their bands' edges up to 13.

```elixir
mpg
|> Plot.new(%{x: :displ, y: :hwy})
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> Plot.geom_smooth(span: 0.8)
|> Plot.facet_wrap(:drv)
|> render.()
```

## Logistic regression

`binomial_smooth()` is a plain R function returning a `geom_smooth()` layer that fits a logistic
regression with `glm()`. The `rpart` package's `kyphosis` records children who had spinal
surgery: their `Age`, in months, and whether a kyphosis was present afterwards, `Kyphosis`, a
factor of `absent` and `present`.

```r
binomial_smooth <- function(...) {
  geom_smooth(method = "glm", method.args = list(family = "binomial"), ...)
}
# To fit a logistic regression, you need to coerce the values to
# a numeric vector lying between 0 and 1.
ggplot(rpart::kyphosis, aes(Age, Kyphosis)) +
  geom_jitter(height = 0.05) +
  binomial_smooth()
```

Not in Giggly yet: `geom_jitter()` and its `height` option, `geom_smooth()` with `method = "glm"`,
the logistic regression R's `glm()` fits, and its `method.args` option, and the `kyphosis` dataset
from the `rpart` package.

## Values between 0 and 1

```r
ggplot(rpart::kyphosis, aes(Age, as.numeric(Kyphosis) - 1)) +
  geom_jitter(height = 0.05) +
  binomial_smooth()

ggplot(rpart::kyphosis, aes(Age, as.numeric(Kyphosis) - 1)) +
  geom_jitter(height = 0.05) +
  binomial_smooth(formula = y ~ splines::ns(x, 2))

# But in this case, it's probably better to fit the model yourself
# so you can exercise more control and see whether or not it's a good model.
```

`as.numeric(Kyphosis) - 1` makes `absent` 0 and `present` 1. The second fits the logistic
regression to a natural spline of `x` from the `splines` package.

Not in Giggly yet: a whole-column function in a mapping, here `as.numeric(Kyphosis)`, a factor's
level codes, `geom_jitter()` and its `height` option, `geom_smooth()` with `method = "glm"`, the
logistic regression R's `glm()` fits, and its `method.args` and `formula` options, and the
`kyphosis` dataset from the `rpart` package.